## Conclusion


By these metrics, the skill improvements and` baml codequality` seems to have worked. The worst function beats the old skill on cognitive complexity, cyclomatic complexity, NPath, and nesting on all four tasks. Exits were the exception, slightly higher on chess and GTFS. The new skill alone was less consistent.


But, these metrics are only a proxy for code quality. A metric going up or down a few points is not necessarily good or bad. And it is possible agents hillclimbed these metrics (even though the instructions specifically said not to). Agents also ended up writing more functions, so some of the improvements came from splitting up functions and moving complexity around: total exits and nesting mostly went up, and total cyclomatic complexity changed little.


The stronger evidence for us is anecdotal: we read the code, and it just seemed overall better. Here's the same chess logic written with the new skill:


```text
enum   ChessKind   {
Pawn  ,
Knight  ,
Bishop  ,
Rook  ,
Queen  ,
King  ,
}


const   CHESS_DIAGONALS   =   [  ChessStep   { file:   -  1  , rank:   -  1   },   ...  ];
const   CHESS_STRAIGHTS   =   [  ChessStep   { file:   -  1  , rank:   0   },   ...  ];
const   CHESS_RAYS   =   CHESS_DIAGONALS.  concat  (CHESS_STRAIGHTS);


function   candidates  (  self  ,   from  :   int  )   ->   ChessPly  [] {
let   piece   =   self  .board[from]
??   return [];
if   (piece.side   !=   self  .side) {
return   [];
}
match   (piece.kind) {
ChessKind  .  Pawn   =>   self  .  pawn_moves  (from),
ChessKind  .  Knight   =>   {
CHESS_KNIGHT_STEPS.  flat_map  ((  step  )   ->   {
self  .  ray_moves  (from, step,   false  )
})
},
ChessKind  .  Bishop   =>   {
CHESS_DIAGONALS.  flat_map  ((  step  )   ->   {
self  .  ray_moves  (from, step,   true  )
})
},
ChessKind  .  Rook   =>   {
CHESS_STRAIGHTS.  flat_map  ((  step  )   ->   {
self  .  ray_moves  (from, step,   true  )
})
},
ChessKind  .  Queen   =>   {
CHESS_RAYS.  flat_map  ((  step  )   ->   {
self  .  ray_moves  (from, step,   true  )
})
},
ChessKind  .  King   =>   {
CHESS_RAYS
.  flat_map  ((  step  )   ->   {
self  .  ray_moves  (from, step,   false  )
})
.  concat  (  self  .  castle  (from,   true  ))
.  concat  (  self  .  castle  (from,   false  ))
},
}
}


function   legal_moves  (  self  )   ->   Move  [] {
CHESS_SQUARES
.  flat_map  ((  from  )   ->   {
self  .  candidates  (from)
})
.  filter  ((  ply  )   ->   {
self  .  after  (ply).  king_safe  ()
})
.  map  ((  ply  )   ->   {
ply.  external  ()
})
}


```


The code now makes good use of BAML's features and type system. Piece kinds are an enum, and the movement directions are constant arrays. A single` ray_moves` function handles both sliding pieces and one-step pieces.` candidates` has a cognitive complexity of 2, and` legal_moves` just generates the candidate moves, drops the ones that leave the king in check, and converts the rest.


Unfortunately, we can't put a number on our feelings about the code, but we feel that it's a lot closer to what we would've written by hand.


